[32m+[m[32mmonth); an annual rate applies to a year. They must not be mixed: an annual rate must not be[m
[32m+[m[32msubtracted from a daily return. Conversion used here (D-024):[m
[32m+[m[32mr_periodic = (1 + r_annual)^(1/N) − 1. Example: 12.68 % per year ≈ 1 % per month.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**Portfolio return (simple return).** The percentage change in portfolio value over one period,[m
[32m+[m[32mwritten as a decimal: r_t = V_t / V_{t−1} − 1. Example: from 100 to 110 is a return of 0.10[m
[32m+[m[32m(+10 %); from 110 back to 99 is −0.10. Note that +10 % followed by −10 % does not return to the[m
[32m+[m[32mstarting value. A series of n + 1 values gives n returns (D-021).[m
[32m+[m
 **Rebalancing.** Trading to move current weights to target weights.[m
 [m
[31m-**Risk-free rate.** Return on a riskless asset (e.g. T-bills), used in Sharpe-type ratios. Provisional[m
[31m-constant: 0.0.[m
[32m+[m[32m**Risk-free rate.** The return available without taking risk, typically approximated by short-term[m
[32m+[m[32mgovernment bills. Used as the hurdle in the Sharpe ratio: a strategy should earn more than this to[m
[32m+[m[32mcompensate for its risk. The project's config holds it as an annual decimal (provisional constant[m
[32m+[m[32m0.0); it is converted to a periodic rate before use.[m
 [m
 **Risk parity / inverse volatility.** Allocations that equalise each asset's risk contribution, or[m
 weight assets in inverse proportion to their volatility.[m
 [m
[31m-**Sharpe ratio.** (Mean return − risk-free rate) / standard deviation of returns, usually annualised.[m
[31m-A core metric in the abstract. The exact convention is to be fixed in milestone 1.[m
[32m+[m[32m**Sample standard deviation (`ddof=1`).** A measure of spread that divides the sum of squared[m
[32m+[m[32mdeviations by T − 1 rather than T, because the data are a sample. With only one observation it is[m
[32m+[m[32mundefined. Used for volatility and the Sharpe ratio (D-022).[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**Sharpe ratio.** Reward per unit of risk: the average return above the risk-free rate, divided by[m
[32m+[m[32mthe volatility of that excess return, then annualised:[m
[32m+[m[32mS = √N · mean(r − r_f) / std(r − r_f). Higher is better. A Sharpe of 1 means that, on average,[m
[32m+[m[32mthe excess return equals one standard deviation per year. It can be negative. If returns do not[m
[32m+[m[32mvary at all, the ratio is undefined (division by zero), and the code raises an error rather than[m
[32m+[m[32mreporting infinity (D-025). A core metric in the abstract. Limitation: it treats upside and downside[m
[32m+[m[32mswings the same way.[m
 [m
 **Slippage.** The difference between the expected and the actual execution price. Modelled here as[m
 an extra cost in bps (provisional; optional extension).[m
 [m
 **Sortino ratio.** Like Sharpe, but divides by downside deviation only.[m
 [m
[31m-**Transaction cost.** Cost of trading, modelled as a proportion (in bps) of traded notional.[m
[31m-Provisional value: 10 bps (D-007).[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Turnover.** How much the portfolio trades. Provisional definition: sum over assets of[m
[31m-|w_new − w_old| per rebalance, ranging from 0 to 2 (D-011). A core metric in the abstract.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Volatility.** Standard deviation of returns, usually annualised.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Weight (portfolio weight).** Fraction of portfolio value held in an asset.[m
[32m+[m[32m**Transaction cost.** Cost of trading, modelled as a proportion (in bps) of the value traded.[m
[32m+[m[32mProvisional value: 10 bps (D-007). Exactly which measure is charged (traded notional, which[m
[32m+[m[32mexcludes the cash leg, or all-column turnover) is not yet decided (D-031).[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**Trough.** The lowest point of a drawdown, before the portfolio starts to recover. The maximum[m
[32m+[m[32mdrawdown is measured from a peak to its trough.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**Turnover (two-way).** How much the portfolio trades at a rebalance. It is the sum, over all assets[m
[32m+[m[32m(including cash if held), of how much each weight changes: Σ |w_post − w_pre|. It ranges from 0 (no[m
[32m+[m[32mtrade) to 2 (sell everything and buy something else). "Two-way" means both the selling and the buying[m
[32m+[m[32mside are counted: moving 10 % from asset A to asset B gives 0.10 + 0.10 = 0.20. The weights before[m
[32m+[m[32mtrading are the *drifted* weights, not the previous targets. A core metric in the abstract; the[m
[32m+[m[32mdefinition is proposed (D-011, D-030). **Annualised turnover** adds up the turnover of all[m
[32m+[m[32mrebalances and expresses it per year (e.g. 1.2 = 120 % of portfolio value traded per year, counting[m
[32m+[m[32mboth sides).[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**Turnover (one-way).** Half of two-way turnover, i.e. only one side of each trade. It is common in[m
[32m+[m[32mthe literature; always state which convention a number uses.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**Traded notional.** The value of securities actually bought plus sold at a rebalance, as a[m
[32m+[m[32mfraction of portfolio value: the sum of |weight change| over the **non-cash** assets. Cash is[m
[32m+[m[32mwhere trades settle; it is not itself traded. Example with a cash column: buying 10 % of an asset[m
[32m+[m[32mwith cash is traded notional 0.10, but two-way turnover over all columns counts it as 0.20 (asset[m
[32m+[m[32mleg plus cash leg). Switching 10 % between two assets is 0.20 under both. In code:[m
[32m+[m[32m`portfolio_turnover(pre, post, cash_column=j)`. Ordering: one-way ≤ traded notional ≤ two-way. The[m
[32m+[m[32mproposed basis for future transaction costs (D-031, still open).[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**Volatility.** How much returns fluctuate around their average, measured as the (sample) standard[m
[32m+[m[32mdeviation of returns and usually annualised. Higher volatility means a bumpier, riskier ride. It[m
[32m+[m[32msays nothing about direction: steady losses can have low volatility. Example: monthly returns of[m
[32m+[m[32m2 % and 4 % have a monthly volatility of about 1.41 %, or about 4.9 % per year.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**Volatility drag.** The gap between arithmetic and compounded growth caused by ups and downs:[m
[32m+[m[32mapproximately σ²/2 per period. A +10 % gain followed by a −10 % loss leaves you at 0.99, not 1.00.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**Weight (portfolio weight).** Fraction of portfolio value held in an asset. Pre-trade weights are[m
[32m+[m[32mmeasured just before a rebalance (after drift); post-trade weights are the targets just after it.[m
