[32m+[m[32m## Explaining the core metrics[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mAll numbers in this section are **illustrative toy examples** chosen to make the arithmetic easy.[m
[32m+[m[32mThey are not project results and describe no real asset or strategy.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m### Why these metrics matter for this project[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe research question is whether an adaptive RL policy is *useful*, not just whether it makes[m
[32m+[m[32mmoney. A strategy with a higher return can still be worse if it takes far more risk, suffers deep[m
[32m+[m[32mlosses, or trades so much that costs eat the gains. The abstract therefore commits to cumulative[m
[32m+[m[32mreturn, the Sharpe ratio, maximum drawdown and turnover. Every strategy is compared on the same[m
[32m+[m[32mmetrics, data, costs and periods.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThink of the metrics as answering four questions:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m1. **How much?** Cumulative return and annualised return.[m
[32m+[m[32m2. **How bumpy?** Volatility.[m
[32m+[m[32m3. **Worth the risk?** The Sharpe ratio.[m
[32m+[m[32m4. **How painful, and how costly to run?** Maximum drawdown and turnover.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m| Metric | Question it answers | Formula | Better when |[m
[32m+[m[32m| --- | --- | --- | --- |[m
[32m+[m[32m| Portfolio return | How much did the portfolio gain or lose each period? | r_t = V_t / V_{t−1} − 1 | — (input to the others) |[m
[32m+[m[32m| Cumulative return | How much did it gain or lose in total? | V_T / V_0 − 1 | Higher (but see risk) |[m
[32m+[m[32m| Annualised return (CAGR) | What steady yearly growth would give the same result? | (V_T / V_0)^(N/T) − 1 | Higher (but see risk) |[m
[32m+[m[32m| Annualised volatility | How bumpy is the ride? | σ_ann = std(r) · √N | Lower, for the same return |[m
[32m+[m[32m| Sharpe ratio | How much excess return per unit of risk? | √N · mean(r − r_f) / std(r − r_f) | Higher |[m
[32m+[m[32m| Maximum drawdown | What was the worst fall from a high? | min_t (V_t / max_{s≤t} V_s − 1) | Closer to 0 |[m
[32m+[m[32m| Turnover (two-way) | How much did a rebalance trade? | Σ_i \|w_post,i − w_pre,i\| | Lower, for the same performance |[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mN is the number of periods per year (e.g. 12 for monthly data); T is the number of periods[m
[32m+[m[32mobserved. All values are decimals (0.10 = 10 %).[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m### Slide-ready explanations[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**1. Portfolio return.** "We turn the portfolio's value path into period-by-period percentage[m
[32m+[m[32mchanges." *Illustration:* 100 → 110 → 99 gives returns of +10 % and −10 %. The portfolio ends at[m
[32m+[m[32m99, not 100, because returns compound.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**2. Annualised volatility.** "Volatility measures how much returns swing around their average. We[m
[32m+[m[32mannualise it so that daily and monthly results are comparable." *Illustration:* monthly returns of[m
[32m+[m[32m+2 % and +4 % have a monthly sample standard deviation of 1.41 %. Multiplied by √12, that is about[m
[32m+[m[32m4.9 % per year. Volatility measures risk as uncertainty: it does not say whether returns were good[m
[32m+[m[32mor bad.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**3. Sharpe ratio.** "Return above the risk-free rate, per unit of volatility: a fair way to[m
[32m+[m[32mcompare a cautious strategy with an aggressive one." *Illustration:* monthly returns of 2 %, 4 % and[m
[32m+[m[32m0 % with a 1 %-per-month risk-free rate give excess returns of 1 %, 3 % and −1 %. Their mean is 1 %[m
[32m+[m[32mand their standard deviation 2 %, so the monthly Sharpe is 0.5 and the annualised Sharpe is[m
[32m+[m[32m0.5 × √12 ≈ 1.73. *Interpretation:* higher is better; negative means the strategy did worse than the[m
[32m+[m[32mrisk-free rate. If returns never vary, the Sharpe is undefined and we report it as such, not as[m
[32m+[m[32minfinity.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**4. Maximum drawdown.** "The worst loss an investor could have experienced, buying at a peak and[m
[32m+[m[32mselling at the following low." *Illustration:* 100 → 120 → 90 → 110 → 130 → 117 has a maximum[m
[32m+[m[32mdrawdown of −25 % (from 120 to 90), even though the path ends higher than it started. The metric[m
[32m+[m[32mcaptures pain that the final return hides.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**5. Cumulative return.** "How much the portfolio grew or shrank in total, from start to finish."[m
[32m+[m[32m*Illustration:* 100 → 110 → 99 → 121 is 121/100 − 1 = **+21 %**. Multiplying the period growth[m
[32m+[m[32mfactors gives the same answer: 1.10 × 0.90 × 1.222 = 1.21. *Caveat:* it ignores how long it took[m
[32m+[m[32mand how rough the journey was. 100 → 300 → 100 has a cumulative return of 0 %.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**6. Annualised return (CAGR).** "The steady yearly growth rate that would produce the same end[m
[32m+[m[32mresult. It lets us compare a 2-year and a 5-year test fairly." *Illustration:* 100 → 121 over two[m
[32m+[m[32myears gives (1.21)^(1/2) − 1 = **10 % per year**, because 1.10 × 1.10 = 1.21.[m
[32m+[m[32m- *Why not just average the returns?* +50 % then −50 % averages 0 %, but 100 → 150 → 75 actually[m
[32m+[m[32m  lost 25 %. CAGR reports −13.4 % per year, which reflects reality. The gap is called[m
[32m+[m[32m  *volatility drag*.[m
[32m+[m[32m- *Big caveat:* annualising a short window exaggerates. One month at +5 % annualises to about[m
[32m+[m[32m  **+80 % per year**. The formula cannot fix this, so we always state the window length, and we do[m
[32m+[m[32m  not headline annualised numbers for windows shorter than a year (proposed rule, D-029).[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**7. Turnover.** "How much of the portfolio we had to trade at each rebalance. It matters because[m
[32m+[m[32mevery trade costs money." *Illustration:*[m
[32m+[m[32m- Moving from 60/40 to 50/50 trades 10 % out of one asset and 10 % into the other. Two-way[m
[32m+[m[32m  turnover counts both sides: **0.20**.[m
[32m+[m[32m- Selling everything and buying something else is the maximum, **2.0**.[m
[32m+[m[32m- *The subtle point, which is good to explain in a viva:* even if the target allocation never[m
[32m+[m[32m  changes, markets move the weights ("drift"). Suppose the target is 50/50, asset A rises 10 % and[m
[32m+[m[32m  B is flat. The portfolio is now about 52.4 / 47.6, so getting back to 50/50 requires trading[m
[32m+[m[32m  (turnover ≈ **0.048**). Comparing "target vs. previous target" would wrongly say 0. That is why[m
[32m+[m[32m  our function takes the *drifted* pre-trade weights.[m
[32m+[m[32m- *Annualised turnover:* add up the turnover of all rebalances and express it per year. For[m
[32m+[m[32m  example, 12 monthly rebalances of 0.10 each give **1.2 per year**.[m
[32m+[m[32m- *Cash, and the question an examiner may ask:* if cash is one of the columns, buying 10 % of an[m
[32m+[m[32m  asset with cash moves two weights (asset +0.10, cash −0.10). Summing over all columns gives[m
[32m+[m[32m  0.20, but only 0.10 of a security was actually traded. So the function has two measures:[m
[32m+[m[32m  - the default sums every column;[m
[32m+[m[32m  - `cash_column=j` leaves cash out and gives **traded notional**: 0.10 for that purchase, and[m
[32m+[m[32m    0.20 for a 10 % switch between two assets.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m  Rule of thumb: one-way ≤ traded notional ≤ two-way. Which measure transaction costs will use is[m
[32m+[m[32m  decided in the environment milestone (D-031); traded notional is the proposed choice.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m### How to interpret them together[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m- Never present return alone. Pair it with volatility, Sharpe, maximum drawdown and turnover.[m
[32m+[m[32m- A higher Sharpe with a much deeper drawdown is a trade-off, not a clear win.[m
[32m+[m[32m- High turnover makes results sensitive to the cost assumption. A strategy that wins only at zero[m
[32m+[m[32m  cost is not a convincing win.[m
[32m+[m[32m- Metrics from short periods are noisy; differences between strategies need uncertainty estimates[m
[32m+[m[32m  (planned) before being called meaningful.[m
[32m+[m[32m- All results must be stated net of transaction costs, with the period (train / validation / test),[m
[32m+[m[32m  the window length T and the annualisation factor N.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m### Check your understanding (answers below)[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m1. A portfolio goes 100 → 80 → 100. What are its cumulative return and its maximum drawdown?[m
[32m+[m[32m2. Why is volatility multiplied by √12, not 12, for monthly data?[m
[32m+[m[32m3. Why can the Sharpe ratio be "undefined", and what do we report then?[m
[32m+[m[32m4. Two years of data turn 100 into 81. What is the CAGR?[m
[32m+[m[32m5. The target weights are the same at every rebalance. Can turnover still be positive?[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m*Answers:*[m
[32m+[m[32m1. Cumulative 0 %; maximum drawdown −20 % (80/100 − 1).[m
[32m+[m[32m2. Variances add over independent periods, so standard deviations grow with the square root of[m
[32m+[m[32m   time.[m
[32m+[m[32m3. If returns never vary, the denominator is zero. We report "undefined", never infinity.[m
[32m+[m[32m4. (0.81)^(1/2) − 1 = 0.9 − 1 = −10 % per year.[m
[32m+[m[32m5. Yes. Price moves cause the weights to drift away from the target, and rebalancing back requires[m
[32m+[m[32m   trades.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m### Methodological choices to mention if asked[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThese conventions were accepted by the team on 2026-10-13, except where marked open:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m- Sample standard deviation (`ddof=1`). It is unbiased for the variance; the standard deviation[m
[32m+[m[32m  itself is slightly biased low (D-022). Annualisation by √N, with N given explicitly. Which N to[m
[32m+[m[32m  use for daily data (252 or 365) is still open (D-023).[m
[32m+[m[32m- An annual risk-free rate is converted to a periodic rate by compounding, never subtracted from[m
[32m+[m[32m  daily returns directly (D-024).[m
[32m+[m[32m- Undefined values raise an error instead of being reported as infinity (D-025). The zero[m
[32m+[m[32m  threshold (1e-12) was checked against measured floating-point noise.[m
[32m+[m[32m- Drawdown is measured from the first observation onward (D-026).[m
[32m+[m[32m- Cumulative return is V_T/V_0 − 1 (D-028). Annualised return is geometric (CAGR), not[m
[32m+[m[32m  mean × N (D-029).[m
[32m+[m[32m- Turnover is two-way, from drift-adjusted pre-trade weights, with cash as a column (D-011, D-030).[m
[32m+[m[32m  By default it sums all columns; `cash_column` gives traded notional. How trading costs use these[m
[32m+[m[32m  measures is still open (D-031).[m
[32m+[m[32m- Correctness: each metric is checked against hand-calculated examples. In addition, a hand-picked[m
[32m+[m[32m  set of deliberate bugs (for example `ddof=0`, simple instead of geometric conversion, one-way[m
[32m+[m[32m  instead of two-way turnover) was injected into a temporary copy of the code. The tests failed for[m
[32m+[m[32m  every one of them (technical handbook §10.11). This is evidence that the tests catch those[m
[32m+[m[32m  specific mistakes. It is **not** systematic mutation testing and does not prove the tests catch[m
[32m+[m[32m  every possible bug.[m
